 exactly so  zelada and then it's like no it's got to  be locally grown organic imeem Walmart  gives you fair trade coffee Walmart  gives you organic food so what's an  active is gonna do I mean what is the  lab so what are they what are what are  they after because they're not about  saying okay it's got you you got to eat  organic it's now you got to eat organic  and plus and this and that so what are  what are they drugs  to be honest there's a lot of reaction  against globalization I mean reverting  back to your tribe or your local food  chat is a one is one way to stick it to  globalization at the same time there's a  lot of romanticism going on about this I  mean I was raised in the countryside for  me producing food is a job but for a lot  of suburban and urban kids well you know  you put the seed in the ground so many  or it grows oh wow you know the miracle  of life so I suspect there's a lot of  knifed in that respect  and also a profound misunderstanding of  the problems inherent to a local food  supply a limitation and what I mean by  that is that historically people would  starve and be malnourished because they  did not have access to the surplus of  regions that had good years so typically  too bad artists in a row and you would  have a famine famine only disappear  really with the railroads and the steam  ships that allowed to move huge  quantities of food from regions that had  good years to regions that had bad years  what is the what's the role of kind of  localism you know or protectionism  really and it I mean it would be termed  in a different debate how is that how  was that driving food bands and whatnot  I mean in places like your city you've  got the size of cups you know yeah you  know soda cups and things like that  foie gras has production and sales have  been ban in California what what's going  on with that and how does it fit into  homes of your book well the team of the  book I mean it's well it's really I mean  I met reason so like 